[job_20261010_2aa566ec] resolve.decision: Married woman whose family already has an unmarried beneficiary: encoder_wrong. Clause 4(2) (page 2, row 2) and clause 3 (page 1, row 1) list two groups joined by 'तसेच' ('as well as'). The first group is married, widowed, divorced, abandoned and destitute women, with no number limit. The second is 'त्या कुटूंबातील केवळ एक अविवाहित महिला' ('only one unmarried woman of that family'). The word 'केवळ एक' ('only one') qualifies only 'अविवाहित महिला' ('unmarried woman'). So the one-per-family cap restricts unmarried women only. It does not stop a married woman from claiming because an unmarried relative already benefits. The rulebook condition one_unmarried_beneficiary_per_family is written as 'not person.other_unmarried_beneficiary_in_family' and applies to every applicant. That wrongly disqualifies this 40-year-old married woman. She meets every other condition, so she should get Rs 1,500.
