[job_20261010_581b6e06] resolve.decision: Other scheme amount exactly Rs 1,500 after change: still eligible: ambiguous. Both sides agree on eligibility. Clause 3 keeps the other-scheme limit at Rs 1,500, and under the glossary entry for "पेक्षा जास्त" a strict 'more than' test lets an amount of exactly Rs 1,500 pass. The only disagreement is the monthly amount: Rs 600 (engine) versus Rs 2,100 (verifier).

The engine's Rs 600 equals 2,100 minus 1,500. So the engine applies a top-up rule: a woman already receiving money from other schemes gets only the difference up to the scheme benefit. That rule comes from the earlier GRs this document cites (28.06.2024 / 03.07.2024), not from this document. Clause 3 ('all other criteria, eligibility and ineligibility remain as before') arguably keeps the top-up rule alive. Clause 1 raises the base amount to Rs 2,100, so a difference of Rs 600 is one coherent reading.

The verifier read clause 1 literally: 'each eligible woman' is paid Rs 2,100 a month. It did not know about the top-up rule. This document never mentions top-ups, so its literal text supports the full Rs 2,100.

A third reading is also possible. The top-up could have been designed to bring other-scheme beneficiaries up to Rs 1,500 only. On that reading she would still get Rs 0, and only women with no other-scheme income would see the increase.

The document does not settle how the top-up interacts with the new Rs 2,100 amount. The answer changes the monthly payment for every woman who receives other-scheme money, from Rs 0 to Rs 2,100 in this case. Note also that the published rulebook text does not show the top-up formula at all, which should be made explicit whichever reading is chosen.
